## Original Content
<p>As federal borrowing rules change, graduate education has a chance to move beyond sticker price and focus on flexibility, transparency, and long term value. For students, the question is not only how to pay for a degree, but whether the degree is designed to be worth the investment.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://blog.coursera.org/as-u-s-graduate-financing-changes-affordability-by-design-matters-more/">As U.S. graduate financing changes, affordability by design matters more</a> appeared first on <a href="https://blog.coursera.org">Coursera Blog</a>.</p>

## Summary
As U.S. federal borrowing rules change, graduate education must shift focus from sticker price to flexibility, transparency, and long-term value. Students should prioritize evaluating whether degrees are designed to be worth the investment.

## Key Points
- Federal borrowing rules are evolving, impacting graduate financing.
- Affordability by design is more important than traditional sticker price.
- Degrees should emphasize flexibility, transparency, and long-term value.
- Students need to assess the designed worth of their educational investments.


